      "text": "To solve this problem, we must first determine the greatest common divisor (GCD) and least common multiple (LCM) of the pair of numbers. We can accomplish this using the Euclidean algorithm and the concept of the least common multiple.\n\nEuclidean algorithm:\n\n$$\\begin{align}\n18 &= 3(42) - 12 \\\\\n42 &= 3(18) + 6\n\\end{align}$$\n\nWe begin by rewriting\n\n$$18 = 3(42) - 12$$\n\nWe will use this as our initial remainder, or u.\n\n$$\\begin{align}\nu_1 &= 18 \\\\\nv_1 &= 42\n\\end{align}$$\n\nSubstituting these values into the formula,\n\n$$\\begin{align}\nq &= \\left\\lfloor \\frac{u_1}{v_1} \\right\\rfloor \\\\\n&= \\left\\lfloor \\frac{u_1}{v_1} \\right\\rfloor \\\\\n&= \\frac{18}{42} > 0 \\\\\n&=1\n\\end{align}$$\n\nWe take the quocient from the first stage up to the last stage and convert $\\frac{1}{q}$ to $\\frac{1}{1}$. Becuase$\\frac{1}{q}$ is less than 1, this relative position is 0.\n\nWhen we're done, our quocients and relative positions are 2 and 1, respectively.\n\nNext, we take the differences from the 2 stages and convert them to u and v, respectively. Take the unkonwn difference, u2, and the uniqickens u1 and compute a new u = u1-u2.\n\n$$\\begin{align}\nu_2 &= 42 - 1 \\times 18 \\\\\n&= 42 -18 \\\\\n&=24\n\\end{align}$$\n\nNext, we write a new v2 using our relative position, r=1 and computed v1 = 42.\n\n$$\\begin{align}\nv_2 &= 18 - 1 \\times 42 \\\\\n&= 18 - 42 \\\\\n&= -24\n\\end{align}$$\n\nFrom the equations:\n\n$r_1 = 0$\n$r_2 = 1$\n\nu = 2\nv =42 $r=1$\n$u = 42-2 \\times 18 24$\n\n$v=18-42+8$ = 24\n\nThe GCD of 18 and 42 is_______.\nThe LCM of 18 and 42 is 126.\n\nTherefore, the final answer is 126.}}",
