      "text": "To find the product of the greatest common divisor (GCD) and the least common multiple (LCM) of \\(18\\) and \\(42\\), we first need to determine their prime factorizations.\n\nThe prime factorization of \\(18\\) is:\n\\[\n18 = 2 \\times 3^2\n\\]\n\nThe prime factorization of \\(42\\) is:\n\\[\n42 = 2 \\times 3 \\times 7\n\\]\n\nNext, we identify the GCD and LCM using these prime factorizations.\n\n**Step 1: Determine the GCD:**\n\nThe GCD is the product of the lowest power of each common prime factor in the factorizations of \\(18\\) and \\(42\\). The common prime factors are \\(2\\) and \\(3\\).\n\n- The lowest power of \\(2\\) in both factorizations is \\(2^{1}\\).\n- The lowest power of \\(3\\) in both factorizations is \\(3^{1}\\).\n\nThus, the GCD is:\n\\[\n\\text{GCD} = 2^1 \\times 3^1 = 2 \\times 3 = 6\n\\]\n\n**Step 2: Determine the LCM:**\n\nThe LCM is the product of the highest power of each prime factor that appears in the factorizations of \\(18\\) and \\(42\\). The prime factors are \\(2\\), \\(3\\), and \\(7\\).\n\n- The highest power of \\(2\\) is \\(2^2\\).\n- The highest power of \\(3\\) is \\(3^2\\).\n- The highest power of \\(7\\) is \\(7^1\\).\n\nThus, the LCM is:\n\\[\n\\text{LCM} = 2^2 \\times 3^2 \\times 7 = 4 \\times 9 \\times 7\n\\]\n\n**Step 3: Calculate the product of the GCD and LCM:**\n\nFirst, calculate the LCM:\n\\[\n4 \\times 9 \\times 7 = 36 \\times 7 = 252\n\\]\n\nNow, calculate the product of the GCD and LCM:\n\\[\n\\text{GCD} \\times \\text{LCM} = 6 \\times 252 = 1512\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the product of the greatest common divisor and the least common multiple of \\(18\\) and \\(42\\) is \\(\\boxed{1512}\\).",
